1. Core Concepts
Ensemble Method: Combining multiple machine learning models (base learners) to create a single, more robust prediction. This reduces variance (overfitting) and improves accuracy.

Bagging (Bootstrap Aggregating): Training individual trees in parallel on different random subsets of the data sampled with replacement. Predictions are then aggregated.

Feature Randomness: At each node split, the tree can only choose from a random subset of features. This decorrelates the trees, making the forest more diverse.

2. Decision Trees vs. Random Forest
Single Tree: High variance, prone to overfitting, highly sensitive to data noise, but very easy to interpret.

Random Forest: Low variance, robust against noise, higher accuracy, but acts as a "black box" (harder to interpret).

3. Aggregation & Hyperparameters
Prediction Methods
Classification: Majority Voting (the class with the most votes wins).

Regression: Averaging (the mathematical mean of all tree outputs).

Key Hyperparameters
n_estimators: Number of trees in the forest.

max_depth: Maximum depth of each individual tree.

max_features: Size of the random subset of features considered at each split.

4. Feature Importance & Pros/Cons
Feature Importance: Ranks variables based on how much they reduce uncertainty (impurity) across the forest, helping identify which inputs matter most.

Pros & Cons
Strengths: High accuracy out-of-the-box, handles mixed data types well, resistant to overfitting.

Weaknesses: Computationally slow with large datasets, high memory consumption, loss of easy interpretability.
